\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Heat convolution and retained boundary information}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{Nonnegative central probability densities on a compact
group are closed under convolution: Tonelli gives mass one
and nonnegativity, and simultaneous conjugation of both
integration variables gives centrality. The preceding
\ref{fdip-00MZ} shows that the SU(2) Wilson subfamily is
not closed. It does not obstruct this larger family.}

\p{The [connected-group heat kernels](fdip-00ML) form a
different one-parameter family, with
##{k_s*k_t=k_{s+t},\qquad
 a_\rho(t)=\ue^{-t c_\rho /2}.}
Thus two-dimensional heat-kernel face merging adds the
area or heat-time parameters. Migdal's recursion gives
the character-level calculation
\citet{§2, pp. 415–416}{migdal1975recursion}.
Lévy proves compatibility under graph subdivision in
\citet{Theorem 1.6.1, pp. 11–14 of the arXiv version}{levy2003yangmills}.
His surface-sewing theorem retains the stated boundary
conditioning
\citet{Theorem 5.1.1, pp. 75–77 of the arXiv version}{levy2003yangmills};
Theorem 5.3.1, pp. 80–84, explains why the two pieces'
invariant sigma-algebras alone can omit information needed
for non-Abelian sewing. These are surface results with
their actual conditional data.}

\p{The [factorized boundary integral](fdip-00MV) keeps its
matching boundary variable before integration. For arbitrary
finite graphs, including SU(3) link configurations, the
general conclusion is the [effective gauge weight](fdip-00MX)
and its exact composition identity. A claim that this pair
has only local plaquette terms, or only a single Wilson
coupling, needs additional proof for the chosen map.
Neither the two-dimensional heat identity nor the SU(2)
nonclosure calculation supplies that claim in higher
dimensions.}
